## Why This Is Asked
Tests expertise in fully dynamic Li Chao trees, offline lifetime decomposition, and practical handling of duplicates in production settings.

## Key Concepts
- **Segment tree over time** for lifetime interval decomposition
- **Per-node Li Chao trees** for minimum query aggregation
- **Path-query traversal** across O(log N) nodes
- Memory-time trade-offs for handling large N and duplicates

## Code Example
```javascript
class LiChao {
  insert(line) { /* insert line into this node's Li Chao tree */ }
  query(x) { /* return minimum y at x across stored lines */ }
}

class DynamicLineSet {
  constructor(N) {
    this.segmentTree = new SegmentTree(N);
  }

  addLine(m, b, id, insertTime) {
    const interval = [insertTime, Infinity];
    this.segmentTree.addInterval(interval, { m, b, id });
  }

  removeLine(id, removeTime) {
    // Update the line's interval end time in the segment tree
    this.segmentTree.closeInterval(id, removeTime);
  }

  queryMin(x, queryTime) {
    return this.segmentTree.queryPath(queryTime)
      .reduce((min, lichao) => Math.min(min, lichao.query(x)), Infinity);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| addLine | O(log N) | O(log N) per line |
| removeLine | O(log N) | - |
| queryMin | O(log² N) | - |

## Edge Cases
- **Identical slopes**: Stored separately with unique ids; Li Chao tree handles min correctly
- **Immediate removal**: Assigned to leaf node only
- **Large N**: Memory scales with O(N log N) total line-node assignments

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of order statistics in dynamic multisets with duplicates and efficient prefix queries—skills fundamental to database indexing and ranking problems.

## Key Concepts
- **Balanced BST with per-node counts and subtree sizes** enables O(log n) operations
- **Handling duplicates via dupCount** avoids unnecessary node creation
- **getRank as a prefix-count query** is analogous to order statistics

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(key) {
    this.key = key;
    this.dup = 1;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
  }

  get size() {
    return this.dup + (this.left?.size ?? 0) + (this.right?.size ?? 0);
  }
}

class Treap {
  constructor() {
    this.root = null;
  }

  insert(key) {
    this.root = this._insert(this.root, key);
  }

  _insert(node, key) {
    if (!node) return new Node(key);
    if (key === node.key) {
      node.dup++;
    } else if (key < node.key) {
      node.left = this._insert(node.left, key);
    } else {
      node.right = this._insert(node.right, key);
    }
    return node;
  }

  erase(key) {
    this.root = this._erase(this.root, key);
  }

  _erase(node, key) {
    if (!node) return null;
    if (key === node.key) {
      node.dup--;
      if (node.dup === 0) return this._merge(node.left, node.right);
    } else if (key < node.key) {
      node.left = this._erase(node.left, key);
    } else {
      node.right = this._erase(node.right, key);
    }
    return node;
  }

  _merge(left, right) {
    if (!left || !right) return left ?? right;
    // Simplified: in a real Treap, merge by priority
    return this._bstMerge(left, right);
  }

  _bstMerge(left, right) {
    if (!left) return right;
    if (!right) return left;
    const node = left;
    node.right = this._bstMerge(node.right, right);
    return node;
  }

  getRank(v) {
    return this._getRank(this.root, v);
  }

  _getRank(node, v) {
    if (!node) return 0;
    if (v < node.key) {
      return this._getRank(node.left, v);
    }
    const leftSize = node.left?.size ?? 0;
    return leftSize + node.dup + this._getRank(node.right, v);
  }
}
```

